<<<
mussuiman. PI. -ans, not -en, the last syllable not being the English word man. It is perhaps to impartial dislike of the incorrect -men & the queer -mans that the comparative disuse of m. is due;
===
**mussulman.** Pl. -*ans*, not -*en*, the last syllable not being the English word *man*. It is perhaps to impartial dislike of the incorrect -*men* & the queer -*mans* that the comparative disuse of *m.* is due;
>>>

<<<
the plural is needed at least as often as the singular, & Manomeran, Mohammedan, & MOSLEM, being resorted to for the plural, get the preference in the singular also.
===
the plural is needed at least as often as the singular, & ^^MAHOMETAN^^, *Mohammedan*, & ^^MOSLEM^^, being resorted to for the plural, get the preference in the singular also.
>>>

<<<
must) (need. The following questions with their positive & negative answers illustrate a point of idiom — Must it be so? Yes, it must; No, it need not./Need I do it? No, you need not; Yes, you must.
===
**must) (need.** The following questions with their positive & negative answers illustrate a point of idiom — *Must it be so? Yes, it must; No, it need not.*/*Need I do it? No, you need not; Yes, you must.*
>>>

<<<
+ mustachio. Pl.-os, see -o(z)s 4. M. is now archaic for moustache, but the adjective derived from it is often preferred to the other; spell mustachio’d, see -ED & ’D.
===
**mustachio.** Pl. -*os*, see -o(E)s 4. *M.* is now archaic for *moustache*, but the adjective derived from it is often preferred to the other; spell *mustachio’d*, see -^^ED^^ & ^^’D^^.
>>>

